          "text": "{\"persuasion\":\"PERSUADED\",\"support_scope\":\"FULL\",\"rationale\":\"The entry’s central identification is stated directly in 1 John 3:4 in the supplied KJV, while Romans 4:15, 3:20, and 7:7 independently explain the law–transgression relationship: law identifies prohibited conduct, gives knowledge of sin, and makes transgression cognizable. The strongest alternative treats sin more broadly as rebellion, alienation, a fallen condition, and an enslaving power. That broader account supplies important dimensions of sin but does not displace transgression of divine law as its moral core; indeed, the common interpretation expressly includes that identification. The entry’s concise definition therefore explains its cited evidence more directly and with fewer additional assumptions.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Sin is the transgression of divine law.\",\"book_secondary_elaborations\":[\"Scripture itself defines sin as transgression of the law.\",\"Where there is no law, there is no transgression.\",\"The law gives knowledge of sin.\",\"Righteousness is the opposite of sin.\"],\"unsupported_glossary_assertions\":[],\"chapter_only_objections\":[\"The chapter’s claims about the continuing scope and precise applicability of the law go beyond the glossary entry and remain doctrinally contestable.\",\"The chapter’s identification of Paul in Romans 7 with the unintentional sinner of Numbers 15 is a broader interpretive argument not established by the glossary entry.\",\"The claim that denying the law necessarily refuses Christ’s atonement or insults the Spirit of grace is a chapter-level application, not part of the entry.\",\"The chapter’s detailed conditions concerning repentance, restitution, forgiveness, and retained mercy are outside the glossary judgment.\"],\"strongest_counter_interpretation\":\"Sin is the broader reality of opposition to God—rebellion, estrangement, corruption, and bondage—expressed through unlawful acts but not exhaustively reducible to those acts, since Scripture can portray sin as a reigning or indwelling power and as an Adamic condition.\",\"counter_core_identification\":\"Sin is opposition to God encompassing unlawful conduct as well as an enslaving and corrupting condition.\",\"counter_relation_to_book_core\":\"SAME_CORE_BROADER\",\"material_core_denial\":\"The counter denies that transgression of law exhausts every biblical presentation of sin, but it does not deny that transgression of divine law is sin or that it identifies sin’s central moral content.\",\"counter_case\":[\"Romans 5–7 portrays sin as entering, reigning, dwelling, deceiving, and enslaving, language broader than a list of discrete transgressions.\",\"Genesis 3 and Romans 5 support understanding sin as a fallen human condition associated with Adam, corruption, and death.\",\"Isaiah 59:2 and related texts emphasize relational estrangement from God.\",\"The broader account accommodates legal, relational, and dominion imagery under one theological category.\"],\"book_explanatory_advantages\":[\"It closely reproduces the explicit wording of 1 John 3:4 in the supplied KJV.\",\"Romans 4:15 directly supports the assertion that transgression presupposes law.\",\"Romans 3:20 and 7:7 directly explain how law supplies knowledge of sin by identifying prohibited conduct.\",\"It gives the broader rebellion account concrete moral content: rebellion is manifested and identified through violation of God’s commands.\",\"It requires no denial of Scripture’s personification of sin; an unlawful disposition or pattern can be portrayed as an enslaving power without changing the basic moral identification.\"],\"counter_explanatory_advantages\":[\"It better foregrounds passages where sin is personified as a ruler, master, or indwelling power.\",\"It accommodates Adamic and corporate dimensions of sin more explicitly than the short glossary definition.\",\"It captures relational consequences such as alienation from God and the universal condition of bondage and death.\"],\"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\":[],\"comparative_winner\":\"BOOK\",\"decisive_sources\":[\"1 John 3:4 — expressly identifies sin with transgression of the law in the supplied KJV.\",\"Romans 4:15 — states that where there is no law, there is no transgression.\",\"Romans 3:20 — states that knowledge of sin comes by the law.\",\"Romans 7:7 — illustrates that the commandment identifies coveting as sin.\"],\"unresolved_objections\":[\"The entry is compressed and does not expressly describe sin’s biblical presentation as an enslaving power, inherited condition, debt, defilement, or relational rupture.\",\"The KJV phrase “transgression of the law” renders Greek anomia, often translated more broadly as “lawlessness”; this may caution against restricting the definition solely to violation of formally codified commands.\",\"Calling righteousness the exact opposite is broadly scriptural but less directly demonstrated by the four references printed with the entry than the law–sin identification itself.\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
